    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d480">

                <pb facs="0596" n="586" sameAs="frus:frus1948v07/pg586"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">893.00B/11–1748</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Consul General at Hong Kong</hi> (<hi
                        rendition="#italic">Hopper</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to the Ambassador
                        in China</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic">Stuart</hi>)<anchor n="24"
                        xml:id="d480-f1"/>
                </head>
                <opener>
                    <seg rendition="#float-left">No. 70</seg>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Hong Kong</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1948-11-17">November 17,
                        1948</date>.</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>
                    <hi rendition="#small-caps">Sir</hi>: I have the honor to enclose a copy of a
                    translation of a message<anchor n="25" xml:id="d480-f2"/> dated November 15,
                    1948, from the Kuomintang Revolutionary Committee to Kmt members in China. This
                    message appeared in the November 16, 1948 edition of the CCP organ <hi
                        rendition="#italic">Hua Shang Pao</hi>, and was included in this office’s
                    Chinese Press Review of that date.</p>
                <p>The message is, on the whole, relatively mild, and attacks the alleged intention
                    of the Generalissimo to place China under United States Government rule, in
                    contrast to customary direct charges against American imperialistic aims in
                    China. A note of confusion is sounded when the Marshal exhorts all loyal Kmt
                    members immediately to rise and act, followed by instructions to “stick to your
                    post, but at the <pb facs="0597" n="587" xml:id="pg587"/> same time work for
                    Chiang Kai-shek’s downfall”. Kmt military men are urged to follow the example of
                    generals who led their forces over to the Communists.</p>
                <p>Respectfully yours,</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">George</hi> D. <hi rendition="#small-caps"
                            >Hopper</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d480-f1" n="24" type="source" xml:id="d480-f1-content">
                <p>Copy transmitted to the Department by the Consul General without covering
                    despatch; received December 3.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d480-f2" n="25" xml:id="d480-f2-content">
